IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ea-core`
IMPORT `ea-definitions`
IMPORT `sgia-interpretation-time`

-- Employment Act 1968 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation
-- "version in force from 5/12/2025" -- LAYER 1, PART 4 (a): ss 35 to 41A, which employees
-- Part 4 reaches, the rest day, work on a rest day, hours of work, shift workers, the week
-- and the Commissioner's powers to exempt.
--
-- Source text: a plain-text rendering made by a converter script from Singapore
-- Statutes Online (inputs/EmA1968.txt).  It is not the official text.  Every
-- `-- src:` line copies a line of that file (script-generated); every quotation in
-- a FALSE-kind or NOTHING-kind comment is a phrase of it (checked by a script).
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act gives in its
-- own words.  NOTHING means the Act does not answer on these facts and is never to
-- be read as no.  "marginal (a)" or "marginal (b)" marks the branches that rest on
-- one of the two readings for a lawyer (NOTES.md).
--
-- SECTION 35 COMES FIRST.  Sections 36 to 41A speak of "an employee".  Section 35 says which
-- employees Part 4 applies to.  The functions below do not repeat that test: the caller (the
-- seat does) asks `s 35 Part 4 applies to the employee` first and goes on only if it is JUST TRUE.
--
-- THE HOUR LIMITS FOR "OVERTIME".  The keel's `s 2(1) "overtime"` takes "the limit of hours
-- specified in Part 4" as a MAYBE NUMBER.  The answers are `s 38(1) the limit of hours in one day`,
-- `s 38(1) the limit of hours in one week` and `s 40(1) the limit of hours in one week` below.

§ `Employment Act 1968 -- Part 4 Rest days, hours of work and other conditions of service, ss 35 to 41A`

§§ `s 35 Application of this Part to certain workmen and other employees`

-- The monthly salary measured against the ceiling: "excluding overtime payments, bonus payments,
-- annual wage supplements, productivity incentive payments and any allowance however described".
-- The keel's salary already leaves out a travelling allowance (s 2(1) "salary"), so only the other
-- allowances are taken off here.  The amounts must be for a month.
-- src:1753 | 35. The provisions of this Part apply —
-- src:1755 | (a) to workmen who are in receipt of a salary not exceeding
-- src:1756 | $4,500 a month (excluding overtime payments, bonus
-- src:1757 | payments, annual wage supplements, productivity
-- src:1758 | incentive payments and any allowance however
-- src:1759 | described) or such other amount as the Minister may
-- src:1760 | prescribe; and
-- src:1762 | (b) to every employee (other than a workman or a person
-- src:1763 | employed in a managerial or an executive position) who
-- src:1764 | receives a salary not exceeding $2,600 a month (excluding
-- src:1765 | any overtime payment, bonus payment, annual wage
-- src:1766 | supplement, productivity incentive payment and any
-- src:1767 | allowance however described) or such other amount as
-- src:1768 | the Minister may prescribe.
@ref s 35 (src:1753-1768)
GIVEN pay IS A `Pay for a period`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 35 the monthly salary measured against the ceiling` MEANS
    IF pay's `the amounts are for` EQUALS `Pay is fixed for each month`
    THEN JUST (`s 2(1) "salary" the amount` pay MINUS sum (LIST pay's `overtime payments`, pay's `bonus payments or annual wage supplements`, pay's `productivity incentive payments`, pay's `food allowances`, pay's `housing allowances`, pay's `allowances of any other kind`))
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the ceilings are stated "a month" and the text does not say how to restate another basis of pay as a month
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Seam S1: the answer asked for by s 88(4A) (ea2), "a workman mentioned in section 35(a)".
@ref s 35(a) (src:1755-1760)
GIVEN `the person is a workman` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the monthly salary measured against the ceiling` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the amount the Minister has prescribed in substitution for $4,500` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 35(a) the workman is one to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of section 35(a)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "workmen who are in receipt of a salary not exceeding $4,500 a month" -- a person who is not a workman is outside paragraph (a) (marginal (a))
        IF NOT `the person is a workman` THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `the monthly salary measured against the ceiling`
            -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the salary measured against the ceiling is not known
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST salary THEN JUST (salary AT MOST ceiling)
    WHERE
        ceiling MEANS
            CONSIDER `the amount the Minister has prescribed in substitution for $4,500`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN 4500
            WHEN JUST c THEN c

-- Seam S2: the answer asked for by s 88(4A) (ea2), "an employee to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of
-- section 35(b)".  The managerial-or-executive fact has the name the plan fixes (seam S16).
@ref s 35(b) (src:1762-1768)
GIVEN `the person is a workman` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employee is employed in a managerial or an executive position` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the monthly salary measured against the ceiling` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the amount the Minister has prescribed in substitution for $2,600` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 35(b) the employee is one to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of section 35(b)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "every employee (other than a workman or a person employed in a managerial or an executive position)" -- marginal (a)
        IF `the person is a workman` OR `the employee is employed in a managerial or an executive position` THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `the monthly salary measured against the ceiling`
            -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the salary measured against the ceiling is not known
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST salary THEN JUST (salary AT MOST ceiling)
    WHERE
        ceiling MEANS
            CONSIDER `the amount the Minister has prescribed in substitution for $2,600`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN 2600
            WHEN JUST c THEN c

@ref s 35 (src:1753-1768)
GIVEN `the answer of s 35(a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the answer of s 35(b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 35 Part 4 applies to the employee` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (`the answer of s 35(a)` EQUALS JUST TRUE) OR (`the answer of s 35(b)` EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN JUST TRUE
        IF (`the answer of s 35(a)` EQUALS JUST FALSE) AND (`the answer of s 35(b)` EQUALS JUST FALSE) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): one of the two paragraphs gave no answer and the other did not say yes
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `s 36 Rest day`

-- src:1774 | 36.—(1) Every employee must be allowed in each week a rest day
-- src:1775 | without pay of one whole day which must be Sunday or such other
-- src:1776 | day as the employer may determine from time to time.
@ref s 36(1) (src:1774-1776)
GIVEN `the employee was allowed in the week a rest day of one whole day without pay` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the rest day was a Sunday or another day the employer determined` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 36(1) the employer has allowed the employee the rest day in the week` MEANS
    `the employee was allowed in the week a rest day of one whole day without pay` AND `the rest day was a Sunday or another day the employer determined`

-- src:1778 | (2) The employer may substitute any continuous period of 30 hours
-- src:1779 | as a rest day for an employee engaged in shift work.
@ref s 36(2) (src:1778-1779)
GIVEN `the employee is engaged in shift work` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the length in hours of the continuous period substituted as a rest day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 36(2) the employer may substitute the continuous period as a rest day` MEANS
    IF `the employee is engaged in shift work` AND (`the length in hours of the continuous period substituted as a rest day` AT LEAST 30)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "any continuous period of 30 hours" for "an employee engaged in shift work" (a period longer than 30 hours is taken to contain one: marginal (b))
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:1781 | (3) Where in any week a continuous period of 30 hours
-- src:1782 | commencing at any time before 6 p.m. on a Sunday is substituted
-- src:1783 | as a rest day for an employee engaged in shift work, the rest day is
-- src:1784 | deemed to have been granted within the week even though the period
-- src:1785 | of 30 hours ends after the week.
@ref s 36(3) (src:1781-1785)
GIVEN `the employee is engaged in shift work` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the continuous period of 30 hours begins on a Sunday` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the hour of the day on the Sunday at which the period begins (0 to 24)` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 36(3) the rest day is deemed to have been granted within the week` MEANS
    IF `the employee is engaged in shift work` AND `the continuous period of 30 hours begins on a Sunday` AND (`the hour of the day on the Sunday at which the period begins (0 to 24)` LESS THAN 18)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "commencing at any time before 6 p.m. on a Sunday" -- ruled 2026-11-05 (b): a "Where" not met (not shift work, or a period that did not begin before 6 p.m. on a Sunday)
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:1787 | (4) Where an employee’s rest day is determined by his or her
-- src:1788 | employer, the employer must prepare or cause to be prepared a roster
-- src:1789 | before the commencement of the month in which the rest days fall
-- src:1794 | informing the employee of the days appointed to be his or her rest
-- src:1795 | days in the month.
@ref s 36(4) (src:1787-1795)
GIVEN `the employee's rest day is determined by the employer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the roster was prepared` IS A DATE
      `the first day of the month in which the rest days fall` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 36(4) the roster was prepared before the commencement of the month` MEANS
    IF `the employee's rest day is determined by the employer`
    THEN JUST (`the day the roster was prepared` LESS THAN `the first day of the month in which the rest days fall`)
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Where an employee’s rest day is determined by his or her employer"
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `s 36(4) Actor` IS ONE OF
    `s 36(4) the employer`

DECLARE `s 36(4) Action` IS ONE OF
    `s 36(4) prepare a roster informing the employee of the days appointed to be the employee's rest days in the month`

-- The duty of subsection (4) as a regulative rule: the roster must be ready before the first day of
-- the month, so the last day for it is the day before.
@ref s 36(4) (src:1787-1795)
GIVEN `the number of days from now to the first day of the month in which the rest days fall` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `s 36(4) Actor` `s 36(4) Action`
`s 36(4) the employer's duty to prepare the roster` MEANS
    PARTY `s 36(4) the employer`
    MUST `s 36(4) prepare a roster informing the employee of the days appointed to be the employee's rest days in the month`
    WITHIN (`the number of days from now to the first day of the month in which the rest days fall` MINUS 1)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `s 36(4) the employer` BECAUSE "the employer did not prepare the roster before the commencement of the month in which the rest days fall"

§§ `s 37 Work on rest day`

-- src:1799 | 37.—(1) Subject to section 38(2) or 40(2A), an employee must not
-- src:1800 | be compelled to work on a rest day unless he or she is engaged in
-- src:1801 | work which by reason of its nature requires to be carried on
-- src:1802 | continuously by a succession of shifts.
@ref s 37(1) (src:1799-1802)
GIVEN `the employee is engaged in work which by reason of its nature requires to be carried on continuously by a succession of shifts` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the case is one in which section 38(2) or 40(2A) allows the employee to be required to work on a rest day` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(1) the employee may be compelled to work on a rest day` MEANS
    IF `the employee is engaged in work which by reason of its nature requires to be carried on continuously by a succession of shifts` OR `the case is one in which section 38(2) or 40(2A) allows the employee to be required to work on a rest day`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an employee must not be compelled to work on a rest day unless he or she is engaged in work which by reason of its nature requires to be carried on continuously by a succession of shifts" -- marginal (a)
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:1804 | (1A) In the event of any dispute, the Commissioner has power to
-- src:1805 | decide whether or not an employee is engaged in work which by
-- src:1806 | reason of its nature requires to be carried on continuously by a
-- src:1807 | succession of shifts.
@ref s 37(1A) (src:1804-1807)
GIVEN `there is a dispute about whether the employee is engaged in work which by reason of its nature requires to be carried on continuously by a succession of shifts` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(1A) the Commissioner has power to decide whether the employee is so engaged` MEANS
    IF `there is a dispute about whether the employee is engaged in work which by reason of its nature requires to be carried on continuously by a succession of shifts`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "In the event of any dispute" -- "In the event of" read as "where" (assumed, not ruled: fork F-EVENT); ruled 2026-11-05 (b)
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- "Normal hours of work" (subsection (3A)(a)): the usual hours per day agreed, not above the
-- limits that apply under s 38 or 40; 8 hours a day if nothing is agreed.
-- src:1856 | (a) “normal hours of work” means the number of hours of
-- src:1857 | work (not exceeding the limits applicable to an employee
-- src:1858 | under section 38 or 40, as the case may be) that is agreed
-- src:1859 | between an employer and an employee to be the usual
-- src:1860 | hours of work per day; or in the absence of any such
-- src:1861 | agreement, is deemed to be 8 hours a day; and
@ref s 37(3A)(a) (src:1856-1861)
GIVEN `the number of hours agreed between the employer and the employee to be the usual hours of work per day` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the limit of hours in one day that applies to the employee under section 38 or 40` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 37(3A)(a) the normal hours of work per day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the number of hours agreed between the employer and the employee to be the usual hours of work per day`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST 8
    WHEN JUST agreed THEN
        IF agreed AT MOST `the limit of hours in one day that applies to the employee under section 38 or 40`
        THEN JUST agreed
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the agreed hours are above the limits, and the definition counts only hours "not exceeding the limits applicable to an employee under section 38 or 40" (marginal (b))
        ELSE NOTHING

-- "Hourly basic rate of pay" (subsection (3A)(b)): worked out as for s 38 overtime, whose rate is
-- the Fourth Schedule's answer (seam S7).
-- src:1863 | (b) an employee’s “hourly basic rate of pay” is to be calculated
-- src:1864 | in the same manner as for the purpose of calculating
-- src:1865 | payment due to an employee under section 38 for working
-- src:1866 | overtime.
@ref s 37(3A)(b) (src:1863-1866)
GIVEN `the answer of sch 4 hourly basic rate of pay` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 37(3A)(b) the employee's hourly basic rate of pay` MEANS
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the rate is calculated "in the same manner as for the purpose of calculating payment due to an employee under section 38", which sends it to the Fourth Schedule
    `the answer of sch 4 hourly basic rate of pay`

-- The least sum for a rest day worked at the employee's own request.  Hours "or part thereof"
-- over the normal hours count as whole hours.
-- src:1809 | (2) An employee who at his or her own request works for an
-- src:1810 | employer on a rest day must be paid for that day —
-- src:1812 | (a) if the period of work does not exceed half the employee’s
-- src:1813 | normal hours of work, a sum at the basic rate of pay for half
-- src:1814 | a day’s work;
-- src:1816 | (b) if the period of work is more than half but does not exceed
-- src:1817 | the employee’s normal hours of work, a sum at the basic
-- src:1818 | rate of pay for one day’s work; or
-- src:1820 | (c) if the period of work exceeds the employee’s normal hours
-- src:1821 | of work for one day —
-- src:1823 | (i) a sum at the basic rate of pay for one day’s work; and
-- src:1825 | (ii) a sum at the rate of not less than one and a half times
-- src:1826 | the employee’s hourly basic rate of pay for each hour
-- src:1827 | or part thereof that the period of work exceeds the
-- src:1828 | employee’s normal hours of work for one day.
@ref s 37(2) (src:1809-1828)
GIVEN `the period of work in hours` IS A NUMBER
      `the employee's normal hours of work for one day` IS A NUMBER
      `the answer of sch 3 basic rate of pay per day` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the answer of sch 4 hourly basic rate of pay` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `a written order under section 41A(2) directs that the entitlement does not apply` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 37(2) the least sum payable for the rest day worked at the employee's own request` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "subject to" the Commissioner's order under section 41A(2), the entitlement "does not apply"
        IF `a written order under section 41A(2) directs that the entitlement does not apply` THEN NOTHING
        IF `the period of work in hours` AT MOST (`the employee's normal hours of work for one day` DIVIDED BY 2) THEN
            CONSIDER `the answer of sch 3 basic rate of pay per day`
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the basic rate of pay for a day's work is the Third Schedule's answer, which may be none
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST day THEN JUST (day DIVIDED BY 2)
        IF `the period of work in hours` AT MOST `the employee's normal hours of work for one day` THEN `the answer of sch 3 basic rate of pay per day`
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `the answer of sch 3 basic rate of pay per day`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST day THEN
                CONSIDER `the answer of sch 4 hourly basic rate of pay`
                -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the hourly basic rate of pay is the Fourth Schedule's answer, which may be none
                WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
                WHEN JUST hourly THEN JUST (day PLUS (1.5 TIMES hourly TIMES CEILING (`the period of work in hours` MINUS `the employee's normal hours of work for one day`)))

-- The same, for a rest day worked at the employer's request: one day, two days, or two days
-- and the overtime sum.
-- src:1830 | (3) An employee who at the request of his or her employer works on
-- src:1831 | a rest day must be paid for that day —
-- src:1833 | (a) if the period of work does not exceed half the employee’s
-- src:1834 | normal hours of work, a sum at the basic rate of pay for one
-- src:1835 | day’s work;
-- src:1840 | (b) if the period of work is more than half but does not exceed
-- src:1841 | the employee’s normal hours of work, a sum at the basic
-- src:1842 | rate of pay for 2 days’ work; or
-- src:1844 | (c) if the period of work exceeds the employee’s normal hours
-- src:1845 | of work for one day —
-- src:1847 | (i) a sum at the basic rate of pay for 2 days’ work; and
-- src:1849 | (ii) a sum at the rate of not less than one and a half times
-- src:1850 | the employee’s hourly basic rate of pay for each hour
-- src:1851 | or part thereof that the period of work exceeds the
-- src:1852 | employee’s normal hours of work for one day.
@ref s 37(3) (src:1830-1852)
GIVEN `the period of work in hours` IS A NUMBER
      `the employee's normal hours of work for one day` IS A NUMBER
      `the answer of sch 3 basic rate of pay per day` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the answer of sch 4 hourly basic rate of pay` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `a written order under section 41A(2) directs that the entitlement does not apply` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 37(3) the least sum payable for the rest day worked at the employer's request` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "subject to" the Commissioner's order under section 41A(2), the entitlement "does not apply"
        IF `a written order under section 41A(2) directs that the entitlement does not apply` THEN NOTHING
        IF `the period of work in hours` AT MOST (`the employee's normal hours of work for one day` DIVIDED BY 2) THEN `the answer of sch 3 basic rate of pay per day`
        IF `the period of work in hours` AT MOST `the employee's normal hours of work for one day` THEN
            CONSIDER `the answer of sch 3 basic rate of pay per day`
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the basic rate of pay for a day's work is the Third Schedule's answer, which may be none
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST day THEN JUST (2 TIMES day)
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `the answer of sch 3 basic rate of pay per day`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST day THEN
                CONSIDER `the answer of sch 4 hourly basic rate of pay`
                -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the hourly basic rate of pay is the Fourth Schedule's answer, which may be none
                WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
                WHEN JUST hourly THEN JUST ((2 TIMES day) PLUS (1.5 TIMES hourly TIMES CEILING (`the period of work in hours` MINUS `the employee's normal hours of work for one day`)))

-- src:1868 | (4) Subsection (3) does not apply to any employee who is employed
-- src:1869 | by the Government or a statutory body in any of the essential services
-- src:1870 | as defined under Part 3 of the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions)
-- src:1871 | Act 1955, but any such employee who at the request of his or her
-- src:1872 | employer works on a rest day or part thereof must be given a day or
-- src:1873 | part of a day off (as the case may be) in substitution for such a rest day
-- src:1874 | or part thereof.
@ref s 37(4) (src:1868-1874)
GIVEN `the employee is employed by the Government or a statutory body in an essential service as defined under Part 3 of the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions) Act 1955` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(4) subsection (3) applies to the employee` MEANS
    IF `the employee is employed by the Government or a statutory body in an essential service as defined under Part 3 of the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions) Act 1955`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Subsection (3) does not apply to any employee who is employed by the Government or a statutory body in any of the essential services" -- marginal (a)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): subsection (4) says only when subsection (3) does not apply
    ELSE NOTHING

@ref s 37(4) (src:1868-1874)
GIVEN `the employee is employed by the Government or a statutory body in an essential service as defined under Part 3 of the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions) Act 1955` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employee works on a rest day or part of it at the request of the employer` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(4) the employer must give the employee a day or part of a day off in substitution` MEANS
    IF `the employee is employed by the Government or a statutory body in an essential service as defined under Part 3 of the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions) Act 1955` AND `the employee works on a rest day or part of it at the request of the employer`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "any such employee who at the request of his or her employer works on a rest day or part thereof"
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 38 Hours of work`

DECLARE `s 38(2) Case` IS ONE OF
    `s 38(2)(a) accident, actual or threatened`
    `s 38(2)(b) work, the performance of which is essential to the life of the community`
    `s 38(2)(c) work essential for defence or security`
    `s 38(2)(d) urgent work to be done to machinery or plant`
    `s 38(2)(e) an interruption of work which it was impossible to foresee`
    `s 38(2)(f) work to be performed by employees in any industrial undertaking essential to the economy of Singapore or any of the essential services as defined under Part 3 of the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions) Act 1955`
    `s 38(2) some other case`

-- The limit of hours in one day.  8, or 9 where an agreement under the contract of service reduces
-- the hours on some days (paragraph (d)) or limits the working days to 5 a week (paragraph (e)).
-- Nothing where section 38 does not apply to the employee (subsection (9)) or the Commissioner has
-- exempted the employee from section 38(1) (section 41A(1)).
-- src:1878 | 38.—(1) Except as hereinafter provided, an employee must not be
-- src:1879 | required under his or her contract of service to work —
-- src:1881 | (a) more than 6 consecutive hours without a period of leisure;
-- src:1886 | (b) more than 8 hours in one day or more than 44 hours in one
-- src:1887 | week:
-- src:1889 | Provided that —
-- src:1891 | (c) an employee who is engaged in work which must be
-- src:1892 | carried on continuously may be required to work for
-- src:1893 | 8 consecutive hours inclusive of a period or periods of not
-- src:1894 | less than 45 minutes in the aggregate during which he or
-- src:1895 | she must have the opportunity to have a meal;
-- src:1897 | (d) where, by agreement under the contract of service between
-- src:1898 | the employee and the employer, the number of hours of
-- src:1899 | work on one or more days of the week is less than 8, the
-- src:1900 | limit of 8 hours in one day may be exceeded on the
-- src:1901 | remaining days of the week, but so that no employee is
-- src:1902 | required to work for more than 9 hours in one day or
-- src:1903 | 44 hours in one week;
-- src:1905 | (e) where, by agreement under the contract of service between
-- src:1906 | the employee and the employer, the number of days on
-- src:1907 | which the employee is required to work in a week is not
-- src:1908 | more than 5 days, the limit of 8 hours in one day may be
-- src:1909 | exceeded but so that no employee is required to work more
-- src:1910 | than 9 hours in one day or 44 hours in one week; and
-- src:1912 | (f) where, by agreement under the contract of service between
-- src:1913 | the employee and the employer, the number of hours of
-- src:1914 | work in every alternate week is less than 44, the limit of
-- src:1915 | 44 hours in one week may be exceeded in the other week,
-- src:1916 | but so that no employee is required to work for more than
-- src:1917 | 48 hours in one week or for more than 88 hours in any
-- src:1918 | continuous period of 2 weeks.
@ref s 38(1) (src:1878-1918)
GIVEN `by agreement under the contract of service the number of hours of work on one or more days of the week is less than 8` IS A BOOLEAN
      `by agreement under the contract of service the number of days on which the employee is required to work in a week is not more than 5` IS A BOOLEAN
      `section 38(9) takes the employee out of this section` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a written order under section 41A(1) exempts the employee from section 38(1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 38(1) the limit of hours in one day` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 38 "does not apply to employees engaged in the fire services or in work which by its nature involves long hours of inactive or stand‑by employment" (subsection (9)), or the employee is exempt by the Commissioner's written order
        IF `section 38(9) takes the employee out of this section` OR `a written order under section 41A(1) exempts the employee from section 38(1)` THEN NOTHING
        IF `by agreement under the contract of service the number of hours of work on one or more days of the week is less than 8` OR `by agreement under the contract of service the number of days on which the employee is required to work in a week is not more than 5` THEN JUST 9
        OTHERWISE JUST 8

@ref s 38(1) (src:1878-1918)
GIVEN `by agreement under the contract of service the number of hours of work in every alternate week is less than 44` IS A BOOLEAN
      `section 38(9) takes the employee out of this section` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a written order under section 41A(1) exempts the employee from section 38(1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 38(1) the limit of hours in one week` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 38 "does not apply to employees engaged in the fire services or in work which by its nature involves long hours of inactive or stand‑by employment" (subsection (9)), or the employee is exempt by the Commissioner's written order
        IF `section 38(9) takes the employee out of this section` OR `a written order under section 41A(1) exempts the employee from section 38(1)` THEN NOTHING
        -- the 48 hours of paragraph (f) are for the week that follows a short week
        IF `by agreement under the contract of service the number of hours of work in every alternate week is less than 44` THEN JUST 48
        OTHERWISE JUST 44

-- src:1881 | (a) more than 6 consecutive hours without a period of leisure;
-- src:1886 | (b) more than 8 hours in one day or more than 44 hours in one
-- src:1887 | week:
-- src:1889 | Provided that —
-- src:1891 | (c) an employee who is engaged in work which must be
-- src:1892 | carried on continuously may be required to work for
-- src:1893 | 8 consecutive hours inclusive of a period or periods of not
-- src:1894 | less than 45 minutes in the aggregate during which he or
-- src:1895 | she must have the opportunity to have a meal;
@ref s 38(1)(a) (src:1881-1895)
GIVEN `the employee is engaged in work which must be carried on continuously` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the minutes in the aggregate during which the employee must have the opportunity to have a meal` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 38(1)(a) the limit of consecutive hours without a period of leisure` MEANS
    IF `the employee is engaged in work which must be carried on continuously` AND (`the minutes in the aggregate during which the employee must have the opportunity to have a meal` AT LEAST 45)
    THEN 8
    ELSE 6

@ref s 38(1)(a) (src:1881-1881)
GIVEN `the number of consecutive hours the employee is required to work` IS A NUMBER
      `the limit of consecutive hours without a period of leisure` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 38(1)(a) the employee may be required to work the consecutive hours` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an employee must not be required under his or her contract of service to work — (a) more than 6 consecutive hours without a period of leisure" -- marginal (a)
        IF `the number of consecutive hours the employee is required to work` GREATER THAN `the limit of consecutive hours without a period of leisure` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- The proviso grants in its own words: "an employee who is engaged in work which must be carried on continuously may be required to work for 8 consecutive hours inclusive of a period or periods of not less than 45 minutes in the aggregate during which he or she must have the opportunity to have a meal".  A limit above 6 exists only through proviso (c), so the hours between 6 and that limit are inside the grant.
        IF (`the limit of consecutive hours without a period of leisure` GREATER THAN 6) AND (`the number of consecutive hours the employee is required to work` GREATER THAN 6) THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "must not be required" says what is forbidden, not that hours within the limit are allowed
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref s 38(1)(b) (src:1886-1889)
GIVEN `the number of hours the employee is required to work in the day` IS A NUMBER
      `the limit of hours in one day` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 38(1)(b) the employee may be required to work the hours in the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the limit of hours in one day`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 38(1) does not reach this employee (subsection (9) or a written order under section 41A(1))
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST limit THEN
        BRANCH
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "more than 8 hours in one day or more than 44 hours in one week" -- marginal (a)
            IF `the number of hours the employee is required to work in the day` GREATER THAN limit THEN JUST FALSE
            -- The provisos (d) and (e) grant in their own words: "the limit of 8 hours in one day may be exceeded ... but so that no employee is required to work more than 9 hours in one day".  A limit of 9 exists only through them, so hours above 8 and up to 9 are inside the grant.
            IF (limit GREATER THAN 8) AND (`the number of hours the employee is required to work in the day` GREATER THAN 8) THEN JUST TRUE
            -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "must not be required ... more than 8 hours in one day" says what is forbidden, not that the lesser hours are allowed
            OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref s 38(1)(b) (src:1886-1889)
GIVEN `the number of hours the employee is required to work in the week` IS A NUMBER
      `the limit of hours in one week` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 38(1)(b) the employee may be required to work the hours in the week` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the limit of hours in one week`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 38(1) does not reach this employee (subsection (9) or a written order under section 41A(1))
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST limit THEN
        BRANCH
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "more than 8 hours in one day or more than 44 hours in one week" -- marginal (a)
            IF `the number of hours the employee is required to work in the week` GREATER THAN limit THEN JUST FALSE
            -- The proviso (f) grants in its own words: "the limit of 44 hours in one week may be exceeded in the other week, but so that no employee is required to work for more than 48 hours in one week".  A limit of 48 exists only through it, so hours above 44 and up to 48 are inside the grant.
            IF (limit GREATER THAN 44) AND (`the number of hours the employee is required to work in the week` GREATER THAN 44) THEN JUST TRUE
            -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "must not be required ... more than 44 hours in one week" says what is forbidden, not that the lesser hours are allowed
            OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- src:1912 | (f) where, by agreement under the contract of service between
-- src:1913 | the employee and the employer, the number of hours of
